WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.470
Film Courage: When do you start writing everyday?

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:02.999
Jeffrey Reddick, Screenwriter: About 11:00
a.m.

00:00:02.999 --> 00:00:10.471
I usually roll out of bed and have my routine
where I’ll say some prayers, meditate a

00:00:10.471 --> 00:00:16.600
little (not much), read the news, see what’s
going on, and pack up and usually go over

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.380
to the coffee shop and start writing.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.769
But I’m also a night owl, too.

00:00:20.769 --> 00:00:21.970
So I stay up late writing.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:26.630
So it’s sporadic because I am usually working,
juggling like four or five different things

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:28.070
at one time.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:35.050
I’m usually hopping around to make sure
I’m on top of whatever needs to be done

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:36.050
first.

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:37.570
Film Courage: How long are you writing for?

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:42.040
Jeffrey Reddick: It’s on and off pretty
much all day.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:49.070
I mean, I’ll be up late watching TV but
still have my laptop on writing a little bit.

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:53.710
It depends, if I have a writing assignment
then I’ll be more structured in it as far

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:59.470
as I will focus more on that and then try
to like have fun and have a life the rest

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:01.332
of the time that I’m not writing.

00:01:01.332 --> 00:01:03.731
But if I’m in a stage where I’m like working
on four or five different things trying to

00:01:03.731 --> 00:01:08.580
get them off the ground, I’m usually writing
quite a bit.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:09.580
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:12.910
So you got to the coffee shop, do you ever
write here [referring to Jeffrey’s place]?

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:17.520
Jeffrey Reddick: Sometimes but if I stay here
I get very distracted like I want to watch

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.170
TV, I want to take a nap, I’m very comfortable
here.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:26.390
I like to go to the coffee shop because I
get my socializing done too because I have

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:27.420
friends there and stuff like that.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.780
So I like to get out and do that, as well.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.860
Film Courage: Are there a lot of regulars
there, too?

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:31.860
Jeffery Reddick: Yeah.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.250
There’s a lot of the.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:34.580
A lot of writers.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:35.580
Film Courage: Nice.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:36.840
Do they ask you what you’re writing?

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.180
Or no one really asks that they just kind
of know.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.970
Jeffery Reddick: Yeah, people will talk to
you.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:43.890
Especially if you go there a long time and
you’ve made friends with people they kind

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:48.729
of know what you’re up to and if they follow
you on social media and you’re talking about

00:01:48.729 --> 00:01:52.470
stuff, they will ask you about what you’re
doing and things like that.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.229
It’s fun.

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:56.180
It’s very transitory though, too.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:00.911
Because there’s a couple regulars but a
lot of the writers if they move they will

00:02:00.911 --> 00:02:03.880
go to another coffee shop or they’ll go
work at the WGA.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:07.150
It has a space to work at or people rent spaces
now.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:10.920
Which I’m actually thinking about doing
because I think I broke it down and I forgot

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:16.450
what the place is called but there’s a company
where you can go rent an office space (and

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:18.800
can’t remember the name).

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:24.810
But I did the math and it’s like oh well
for a cup of Starbuck’s coffee and a donut

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:28.150
every day, I can actually rent this place
for a month and they have free coffee all

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:29.150
day.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:31.560
But I drink tea so I have to check and make
sure they have tea.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.670
Film Courage: It’s probably not as good
as Starbucks.

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:36.540
Jeffery Reddick: No but it’s cool.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.349
You can get a desk and a computer (you have
your own little spot).

00:02:40.349 --> 00:02:44.230
It does kind of put you in a different mindset
if you’re leaving your own place to go to

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:48.940
work because even if I’m walking to Starbucks,
even if I’m going there to work and I’m

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:55.220
going there to see my friends and I’m going
to be distracted and you never know what’s

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:57.120
going to happen.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:02.360
There is some wisdom to having a writing space
that’s an office.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:04.640
I’ll do that at some point.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.020
I haven’t done that yet.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:11.140
But I’ll have a place where I’ll have
an office that’s just a separate building

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:13.030
aside from where I am living.

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:17.400
Film Courage: Yeah, I was always opposed to
coffee shop writing for the longest time and

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.340
then I noticed that if you have headphones
on and you can still sort of see what’s

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:26.569
going on around you, it’s actually invigorating
(whatever you are working on).

00:03:26.569 --> 00:03:28.750
Even if you’re not screenwriting, you’re
just working on something.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:32.680
Jeffery Reddick: Yeah, you’ve got to have
headphones because they’re important because

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.590
people will obviously start talking to you.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:35.750
Film Courage: Oh that’s true!

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:36.750
It’s a good buffer.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:39.490
It’s a good social buffer to have those
on.

00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:40.760
But also libraries, too.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:45.590
I’m just surprised that it actually does
feed you and I was opposed to it for the longest

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:46.590
time.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:50.700
And I can tell now why people need to get
out of the house.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:55.269
Jeffery Reddick: You need that little segue
from being in your home to your brain going

00:03:55.269 --> 00:03:56.560
“Hey, we’re going to work now.”

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:01.510
And if I’m just going from my bedroom to
the living room it’s hard to just sit over

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:03.180
here and say “Okay, turn on my work brain.”

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:04.610
Because I’m like “Wait, what’s on TV?

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.730
I’m going to take a nap really quick.”

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:12.129
Film Courage: Right or if I see an interesting
article on like Flipboard or Pocket or something

00:04:12.129 --> 00:04:13.870
and then I’m like “Let me just read this.”

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:14.980
And then I’m distracted.

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:20.130
Jeffery Reddick: One you get derailed it’s
kind of hard to get the focus back again.

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:23.029
Film Courage: Would you say you’re very
disciplined when you know that you need to

00:04:23.029 --> 00:04:25.520
get these four things done that you’re pretty
good?

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.110
Jeffery Reddick: Yeah, I think my friends
think I’m more disciplined than I am.

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:30.440
I think I could probably be more disciplined.

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:36.151
I do work hard though but it’s…I guess
I am, but in my brain I’m not disciplined

00:04:36.151 --> 00:04:39.490
enough and my friends are like “You’re
so disciplined.”

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:42.669
I feel like I’d be doing more if I was more
disciplined but I’m usually always juggling

00:04:42.669 --> 00:04:44.360
a lot at the same time.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:48.909
But you always want to make sure that you
don’t have more on your plate than you can

00:04:48.909 --> 00:04:51.090
really dedicate quality time to.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:53.479
I’ve definitely learned the art of saying
no as I’ve gotten older.

00:04:53.479 --> 00:04:56.120
Because when you’re young you want to say
yes to everything because you know these could

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:59.849
be my three opportunities, the few things
I ever get.

00:04:59.849 --> 00:05:01.400
So you always want to be saying yes to everything.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.849
And as you get older you start realizing that
it’s okay to say no to stuff you’re not

00:05:04.849 --> 00:05:05.849
passionate about.

00:05:05.849 --> 00:05:08.930
Or if you don’t think you’ll have the
time to really do it and I’ve done that

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:12.210
where I’m like this really isn’t my thing
and I don’t think I’m the best writer

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:13.210
for it.

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:16.999
I know a lot of writers who will take jobs
just to have a job.

00:05:16.999 --> 00:05:20.580
Unless I think it’s something that I could
do justice to I’m not going to take it on

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:23.779
as a job unless it’s a challenge.

00:05:23.779 --> 00:05:26.349
Sometimes there have been things where people
have brought me projects where I’m like

00:05:26.349 --> 00:05:30.020
this isn’t my wheelhouse but it would be
really fun to try to write it so then I’ll

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:31.020
do that.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:33.210
But it’s got to be something that really
strikes a chord with me.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:38.419
Film Courage: Do you know any writers that
write eight to ten hours a day?

00:05:38.419 --> 00:05:39.539
Jeffery Reddick: Yeah.

00:05:39.539 --> 00:05:40.539
Yeah.

00:05:40.539 --> 00:05:41.539
Film Courage: Okay.

00:05:41.539 --> 00:05:44.249
So I’m assuming they stop for lunch or they
do something.

00:05:44.249 --> 00:05:45.270
Jeffery Reddick: Yeah.

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:51.020
I have a lot of writer friends that if they’re
not on staff they very much treat it like

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:52.020
a 9-5 job.

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:55.219
They’ll get up in the morning, they’ll
do there thing, write until lunch time, they’ll

00:05:55.219 --> 00:05:58.899
take lunch, they will write to 5:00 p.m. and
then they will stop writing for the say.

00:05:58.899 --> 00:06:04.170
And again, I think that’s great if you’re
working on one thing at a time, I think you

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:05.710
have the luxury of doing that more.

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:10.460
I think if you’re trying to do two or three
projects at the same time, then it’s harder

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:12.089
to do that.

00:06:12.089 --> 00:06:13.599
But you can do it.

00:06:13.599 --> 00:06:17.309
You know I certainly have friends that do
it.

